RAWMSTAT.CONF(5)

# NAME

rawmstat.conf - rawmstat runtime configuration

# DESCRIPTION

*rawmstat*(1) uses libconfig syntax.  Configuration describes the status
protocol, textual composition policy, and the blocks that produce status text.

The built-in configuration contains one clock block and is usable without a
configuration file.  In normal mode configuration is layered in this order:

. built-in defaults
. _${SYSCONFDIR}/rawm/rawmstat.conf_ (normally _/etc/rawm/rawmstat.conf_)
. _$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rawm/rawmstat.conf_, when *XDG_CONFIG_HOME* is absolute;
  otherwise _$HOME/.config/rawm/rawmstat.conf_

Later scalar settings override earlier values.  When a *blocks* list is
present it replaces the complete block list from previous layers.

With *rawmstat -c path*, the system and user files are skipped: built-in
defaults are followed by exactly _path_.  A missing explicitly selected file
is an error.  Missing normal system or user files are not errors.

Unknown settings, wrong types, duplicate block names, unsupported protocols,
invalid signal offsets, and invalid UTF-8 configuration strings are fatal
configuration errors.  Use *rawmstat -C* to validate the effective
configuration without opening X or executing commands.

Libconfig *@include* directives are supported.  Relative include paths are
resolved relative to the directory of the configuration file being loaded.

# STATUS

The top-level *status* group controls composition and transport.

*protocol* = "rawm-v1"
	Output protocol.  *"rawm-v1"* is currently the only supported value and
	the built-in default.

*delimiter* = string
	Text inserted between non-empty block results.  The built-in default is
	*" | "*.  The delimiter must be valid UTF-8.  It is not appended before
	the first or after the last emitted block.

Example:

```
status = {
  protocol = "rawm-v1";
  delimiter = " | ";
};
```

## rawm-v1

The final status is a plain UTF-8 string of at most 255 bytes.  In normal X
mode rawmstat writes it to the *\_RAWM\_STATUS\_V1* property of the X root window
with type *UTF8_STRING* and format 8.

The protocol contains text only.  It defines no colors, markup, block IDs,
click routing, producer discovery, or process supervision.  Presentation is
owned by *rawm*(1).  The root *WM_NAME* property is not used.

# BLOCKS

The top-level *blocks* setting is a list of groups.  Blocks are evaluated in
list order and non-empty results are joined with *status.delimiter*.

```
blocks = (
  {
    name = "clock";
    prefix = "";
    command = ("date", "+%a %b %d %H:%M");
    interval = 5;
    signal = 0;
  }
);
```

Each block accepts these settings:

*name* = string
	Required unique block name.  The name identifies the block in diagnostics;
	it is not transmitted by *rawm-v1*.  Empty and duplicate names are errors.

*prefix* = string
	Optional UTF-8 text prepended to the block's captured first line.  Default
	is the empty string.  Prefix plus captured text must fit within the protocol
	limit.

*command* = (string, ...)
	Required non-empty argument vector.  Element zero names the executable;
	the remaining elements are passed as its arguments.  rawmstat calls
	*execvp*(3) directly.  Shell parsing, expansion, redirection, pipelines,
	and globbing do not occur unless a shell is explicitly named in the vector.

*interval* = integer
	Periodic update interval in seconds.  Must be >= 0.  Zero disables periodic
	updates; the block is still executed once at startup and may be refreshed by
	its configured signal.  Scheduling uses a monotonic clock.

*signal* = integer
	Realtime-signal offset.  Must be >= 0 and available on the host.  Zero
	disables signal-triggered updates.  A value _N_ maps to *SIGRTMIN+N*.
	Multiple blocks may intentionally use the same non-zero offset and will all
	be refreshed by that signal.

# COMMAND OUTPUT

rawmstat captures standard output from each block.  Only the first line is
used; subsequent output is discarded.  A trailing carriage return is removed
from the captured line.

The captured line must be valid UTF-8, contain no embedded NUL byte, and fit
within 255 bytes before composition.  The configured prefix is prepended after
capture.  Blocks with an empty resulting string are omitted from delimiter
composition.

Standard error from a block is not captured and remains attached to
rawmstat's standard error.  Command execution errors are reported by rawmstat.

The complete composed status, including prefixes and delimiters, must not
exceed 255 bytes.  If it does, rawmstat reports an error rather than publishing
a truncated protocol message.

# EXAMPLES

A clock and load-average status:

```
status = {
  protocol = "rawm-v1";
  delimiter = "  ";
};

blocks = (
  {
    name = "load";
    prefix = "load ";
    command = ("cut", "-d", " ", "-f", "1-3", "/proc/loadavg");
    interval = 5;
  },
  {
    name = "clock";
    command = ("date", "+%H:%M");
    interval = 30;
  }
);
```

A command that deliberately requires shell syntax:

```
blocks = (
  {
    name = "home";
    command = ("/bin/sh", "-c", "printf '%s' \"$HOME\"");
    interval = 0;
    signal = 1;
  }
);
```

Refresh that block with the platform's numeric value for *SIGRTMIN+1*, for
example from C via *kill*(2) or from a shell that supports realtime signal
names/numbers.

# FILES

_/etc/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	System configuration with the default build-time *SYSCONFDIR*.

_$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	Preferred user configuration when *XDG_CONFIG_HOME* is absolute.

_$HOME/.config/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	Fallback user configuration.

# SEE ALSO

*rawmstat*(1), *rawm*(1), *rawm.conf*(5), *libconfig*(5)

